// deem_incr_static_minmax_u64_fail — THE CELL `deem_incr_diff_harness` CANNOT
// HOLD, AND THE ONE THAT MAKES P5 A CAPABILITY DELETION RATHER THAN A MOVE.
//
// The interpreter is being deleted (P5 of the incremental-deem arc) and its
// incremental engine's answers are being moved to the static side. This file
// pins ONE fact about that move, in the only place it can be pinned: `min`/`max`
// over a `u64` column is a COMPILE-TIME REFUSAL in `deem!`, while the
// incremental engine answers it — and has answered it CORRECTLY since
// 2026-08-05, when `agg_ord_key`/`el_wrap_ord_key` (stdlib/mem/deem/incr.logos:
// 611-615) closed the case where `max` over {1u64, 2^64−1} returned the SMALLER
// value. That fix is pinned by `tests/logos/pass/wql_domain_incr_disagreement`
// at :162-174.
//
// ⚠ SO THE DOMAIN RUNS THE OTHER WAY FROM THE OBVIOUS ONE. On aggregate
// ARGUMENTS the static tier is strictly NARROWER than the tier that is being
// deleted. After P5 the answer this refusal withholds does not exist anywhere in
// the language. Either `el_agg_arg_reaches` is widened before P5, or a
// capability leaves with the interpreter. That is Victor's decision and this
// file does not preempt it; it only makes the choice impossible to make by
// accident.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE AND NOT A `Cell` IN THE HARNESS. A refusal at
// COMPILE time cannot be a runtime value: the query below does not produce a
// wrong answer, it produces no program. It cannot even coexist in the same file
// as the harness's runtime arms. `deem_incr_diff_harness` names this file from
// its header and this file names it back, so a reader of either finds the other.
//
// ⚠ THE REFUSAL IS THE VALUE'S, NOT THE POSITION'S, AND THE SIBLING FILES SAY SO
// IN THREE VOICES. `el_agg_arg_reaches` (stdlib/mem/wql/el.logos:590) admits an
// integer argument iff `el_norm_cast` names the accumulator AND `ElTy.fits`
// says the value survives. For `u64`/`usize` `fits` is FALSE: the cast
// REINTERPRETS, so every value at or above 2^63 arrives negative and the total
// i64 order that `min`/`max` then use is the wrong order. `wql_agg_wide_int_arg_fail`
// is the same refusal for `sum` and `wql_agg_f32_arg_fail` the same for a float
// argument on a different ground. What is NOT refused is the COLUMN: `avg` over
// this same column compiles (its fold writes `(s.v) as f64` itself, `agg_cast`
// at stdlib/mem/wql/rexpr_walk.logos:4246), and grouping, ordering, comparing and
// projecting a `u64` all carry `u64` itself — see `wql_key_own_type_e2e`.
//
// ⚠ THE CONTROL IS IN THE SAME DECLARATION. `mn = min(s.k)` over the `i64`
// column is the FIRST aggregate in the list and is admitted; only the `u64` one
// is refused. If a future change makes this file red by refusing BOTH, the
// diagnostic below will not match and that is the file doing its job — the claim
// is about the argument TYPE, not about `min`.
//
// IF THE STATIC RULE IS WIDENED, THIS TEST GOES RED AND ITS `.expected` MUST BE
// REPLACED BY AN ANSWER AT u64's EXTREMES — not by a compile. The answer to
// write is the one the incremental engine already gives:
// max{1u64, 18446744073709551615u64} = 18446744073709551615.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub k: i64, pub v: u64 }

pub deem extremes(src: &[Rec]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate mn = min(s.k), mx = max(s.v)
      select (key, mn, mx) }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
